# Container entry point fixtures, and the empty --only pattern -- 2026-10-04

One sentence: `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` owns the gate list that every other runner and
CI step mirrors, it had no fixtures of its own, and writing them turned up `--only ""` running
the entire list while printing a verdict that reads exactly like an unfiltered sweep.

The host twin `scripts/verify-gates.sh` carries a `--self-test` suite (47 cases before this
change, 50 after) and derives its gate list by parsing the `gates` array of the container
script, so the list is exercised from the reading end. Nothing exercised the file being read.
Its flag parsing, `--only` filter, preflight, tree checksums, verdict wording and exit codes
had been run by hand, against a real image, one gate per sitting -- which is how the `diff`
defect recorded in `docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log` reached a real
164-second run before anyone saw it.

== 1. The wiring guard is what asked for this

The new file was written before it was wired, and the guard that exists to stop exactly that
said so. Both halves of its message fired on the same path:

$ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py is not invoked by any workflow or by
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh; wire it into a CI step and the `gates` array, or record
    why in EXEMPT
    scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py runs in CI but not in
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh; mirror it into the `gates` array or record what it needs
    in scripts/ci/docker-entry-ci-only.tsv
    check-guard-wiring: 2 problem(s)

The second line is the guard reasoning about a file it can see in `git ls-files` but not in
any workflow, which is the state a new fixture is in for a few minutes. House policy is no
allow list, so the answer was to wire it, not to record it.

== 2. The defect: an empty pattern is not a filter

Two copies of the repository, the same stub `docker` on `PATH`, the only difference being
which revision of the entry point is in `scripts/`. "before" is the version at `HEAD`, "after"
is the working tree. The stub answers `build` and `volume`, and appends one line per `run`, so
the count is how many containers the script actually asked for:

    --- before (git HEAD version = before, working tree = after)
    rc=0  docker invocations=36
        == --only is in effect: 35 of 35 gates selected, this is not a sweep
        all gates passed in stub:1
    --- after (git HEAD version = before, working tree = after)
    rc=2  docker invocations=0
        --only needs a gate label (list them with: scripts/verify-gates.sh --list)

Three separate things are wrong in the "before" block, and the third is the ugly one:

- exit 0. The command refused to filter and then reported success.
- 36 `docker` invocations. One preflight plus every gate in the quick list, which is the run
  the caller least wanted and, at 23 to 25 minutes for `--full`, the most expensive one.
- it printed `all gates passed in stub:1`, not the filtered wording. The summary filter is
  `[ "${#gates[@]}" -ne "${total_gates}" ]`, and an empty pattern selects `total_gates` of
  `total_gates`, so a run started with an explicit `--only` produced the one sentence the
  `--only` design exists to reserve for unfiltered sweeps. This is the property recorded in
  `CONTRIBUTING.md` "Fast local gate loop": a filtered run cannot be quoted back as a sweep.

`--only ""` is not a hypothetical invocation. It is what a loop over an unset variable
produces, and `--only "$label"` with an empty `$label` is one shell typo away from a full
sweep that reports itself as scoped.

== 3. The guard tested the wrong word, on both sides

The container entry point did have a guard, and its own comment states the intent:

    # An empty pattern matches every label, which would make `--only ""` a full sweep.
    if [ $# -lt 2 ] || [ -z "$1" ]; then

`$1` is the literal `--only` at that point in the `while`/`case` loop, so the second test can
never fire. The value is `$2`. The fix is one character, plus the comment that says why the
distinction is there, because the line reads as a harmless redundancy otherwise:

$ grep -n -A4 'value is ..2. here' scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    49:      # value is `$2` here, not `$1`: `$1` is the literal `--only` and can never be empty, so
    50:      # testing it let `--only ""` select every gate and print a verdict claiming a filter was
    51:      # in effect. Measured on 2026-10-04 against a stubbed docker: exit 0, whole list run.
    52:      if [ $# -lt 2 ] || [ -z "$2" ]; then
    53-        echo "--only needs a gate label (list them with: scripts/verify-gates.sh --list)" >&2

The host runner had the mirror-image hole. Its parser refused a missing value but accepted an
empty one, and `matches_only` skips empty lines (`[ -n "$pattern" ] || continue`), so nothing
matched:

$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --only "" --list; echo "rc=$?"
    rc=0

Run mode was less quiet -- `nothing ran: every selected gate was skipped (0 of 37 gates
selected by --only)` and exit 1 -- because that runner aggregates rather than aborts and has a
guard for measuring nothing. `--list` said nothing and exited 0, which is a command that
cannot be distinguished from "this label does not exist yet". Both sides now refuse the empty
value at parse time with the same message, so the flag behaves identically whichever runner
receives it.

== 4. What the fixtures drive

`scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py`, 26 cases. The script under test is copied
byte-identically into a throwaway git repository, because it derives its own `repo_root` from
`$0` and the copy is what makes the fixture hermetic; `PATH` is prefixed with a directory
holding a `docker` that records every invocation it is given. Every assertion is therefore
about what the entry point asked for, not about what this repository's text says:

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py 2>&1 | tail -4
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    Ran 26 tests in 2.007s

    OK

Four classes, one per thing that was only ever checked by eye:

- `FlagTests` -- `--help` reaches no `docker` at all, an unknown argument exits 2, `--only`
  with no value and with an empty value are both refused, a pattern matching no label is an
  error rather than a green run, a label that `--full` appends says so in quick mode and is
  found in full mode, and `--shell` hands the terminal over without running a gate.
- `SelectionTests` -- `--only` reaches exactly one gate (preflight plus one run, and the
  gate it did not name is absent from the log), a fragment reaches all three labels containing
  it, two patterns give the union, the command line arrives without its label attached, a
  filtered run gets the filtered wording and never the sweep wording, and an unfiltered run
  runs the whole list, which pins the size of that list so an entry added or dropped trips it.
- `VerdictTests` -- a failing gate is named and exits 1, a later gate still runs after it, a
  failing gate that prints nothing on stdout is still named, a passing gate that prints
  nothing still ends with a verdict, a failed preflight runs no gate and names
  `safe.directory` as the thing to fix, a tree that moves mid-run prints `UNATTRIBUTABLE`
  *and* a verdict, and a file nobody committed is both counted in the fingerprint and declared
  before the first gate line.
- `MountContractTests` -- every gate run carries the five `--env` entries and the mounts, the
  three cargo volumes are created by name rather than bind-mounted, the checkout is mounted at
  `/src`, the image is built exactly once and before any run, and `target/` exists at the
  moment the first container starts.

The last one is measured from inside the stub rather than asserted about the script text. The
entry point creates `target/` so that the runtime does not create the volume's mount point as
root inside the checkout, and a stub that reports `[ -d ... ]` at the moment `docker run` is
called is the difference between "the mkdir is in the file" and "the directory was there when
the container started".

Two fixture details are load-bearing for the tree-checksum cases. The fixture repository
carries a `.gitignore` with `stub-bin/` and `target/`, because the fake `docker` is not
repository content and a fixture that asserts "the tree was at rest" has to be able to tell
that from the absence of the caveat line. And the stub records one invocation per line,
escaping embedded newlines, because a gate command line is multi-line: the `${bootstrap}`
prefix is three `git config` lines, and a wrapped record splits one run across several lines.

== 5. Mutation matrix

Thirteen anchored single edits, one baseline check first, each mutant reverted before the
next, and both runners compared byte-for-byte against a copy taken before the first edit. An
anchor that fails to match counts as SURVIVED, never as a pass. Killers are the named failing
cases.

    M1  the diff keeps its `|| true` removed (revert of 021b5453)      killed
        FAIL: test_a_tree_that_moves_mid_run_is_unattributable_and_still_gives_a_verdict
    M2  the build output directory is not created first                killed
        FAIL: test_the_build_output_directory_precedes_the_first_run
    M3  the git ownership env entries are dropped                      killed
        FAIL: test_every_gate_run_carries_the_env_the_leak_fixes_depend_on
    M4  the --only guard tests $1 again (today's bug)                  killed
        FAIL: test_an_empty_pattern_is_refused_rather_than_selecting_everything
    M5  an unmatched pattern is warned about instead of refused        killed
        FAIL: test_a_label_that_only_full_mode_appends_says_so
        FAIL: test_a_pattern_matching_no_label_is_an_error_not_a_green_run
    M6  a filtered run uses the sweep wording                          killed
        FAIL: test_a_filtered_run_cannot_be_quoted_as_a_sweep
        FAIL: test_a_silent_passing_gate_still_ends_with_a_verdict
        FAIL: test_a_tree_that_moves_mid_run_is_unattributable_and_still_gives_a_verdict
    M7  a failed preflight does not stop the run                       killed
        FAIL: test_a_failing_preflight_runs_no_gate_and_says_what_to_fix
    M8  the label is passed to the container as part of the command    killed
        FAIL: test_the_command_line_reaches_the_container_without_its_label
    M9  --only stops filtering                                         killed
        12 cases, including every selection case and both verdict-wording cases
    M10 untracked files leave the fingerprint                          killed
        FAIL: test_a_file_nobody_committed_is_counted_and_declared
        FAIL: test_a_tree_that_moves_mid_run_is_unattributable_and_still_gives_a_verdict
    M11 the uncommitted-path caveat is never printed                   killed
        FAIL: test_a_file_nobody_committed_is_counted_and_declared
    M12 --shell runs the gate list                                     killed
        FAIL: test_shell_mode_hands_the_terminal_over_and_runs_no_gate
    M13 the host runner's --only guard tests $1 again                  killed
        not ok an empty --only pattern is refused, not parsed (expected exit 2, got 1)
        not ok naming the flag instead of selecting nothing (no line matching: needs a gate label)

    13/13 mutants killed, 0 survived

M1 is a verbatim revert of the fix recorded in
`docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log`, and it is now caught by a fixture in
under a tenth of a second instead of by a 164-second container run. M4 and M13 are reverts of
the fix this log is about, one per runner: the container mutant is killed by the new fixture,
the host mutant by the three new `--self-test` cases. M6 is killed by three cases rather than
one, which is the sign that the wording is asserted wherever a verdict is asserted.

The host self-test gained those three cases and no more; the whole suite is 50:

$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test 2>&1 | grep -B1 -A2 'empty --only pattern'
    ok    --only without a value is refused
    ok    an empty --only pattern is refused, not parsed
    ok    naming the flag instead of selecting nothing
    ok    and no gate ran on the way to the error

$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test 2>&1 | tail -1
    verify-gates self-test: 50/50 cases pass

== 6. Wiring, and the fixtures run inside the image

The new entry sits next to the one that reads the array, so the two ends of the same list are
adjacent in both places the list is mirrored -- the `gates=()` array and the guard step of the
`docs localization` job:

  "host gate runner self-test: bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test"
  "container runner fixtures: python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py"

Running the new gate inside the image is a different question from running it on the host: the
image is a stock Debian bookworm with no cargo volumes needed and no repository state to
inherit, so it answers whether the fixtures assume anything about a developer machine.

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen bash scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only 'container runner fixtures'
    == --only is in effect: 1 of 36 gates selected, this is not a sweep

    == preflight: container can read the repository
    preflight: OK

    == container runner fixtures
    test_a_label_that_only_full_mode_appends_says_so (__main__.FlagTests) ... ok
    [...]
    test_the_first_gate_to_fail_does_not_stop_the_others (__main__.VerdictTests) ... ok

    ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    Ran 26 tests in 2.244s

    OK

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 1 of 36 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

The middle is the other 24 case names, each `ok`; elided for length, not for omission. The
same empty pattern that used to start 36 containers is then refused against the real daemon,
and the container list confirms nothing was created:

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen bash scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only ""
    --only needs a gate label (list them with: scripts/verify-gates.sh --list)

$ docker ps -a --filter ancestor=chaos-verify:frozen --format '{{.ID}}' | wc -l
    0

That refusal happens before `docker build`, so it costs no image resolution either, which is
the difference between the parser catching this and the filter catching it.

== 7. What this does not cover

The stub is not `dockerd`. It answers four subcommands and records argv, so these fixtures
cannot see anything about what happens inside a container: no image build, no volume contents,
no `/src` mount actually resolving, no gate exit status that the real tools produce. What they
own is the other half -- every decision the entry point makes before and after handing a
command line to the runtime, which is exactly the half that had no fixtures and that two
defects had already been found in.

The real sweep remains the evidence for the container side, and it stays expensive on purpose:
`--full` covers `cargo check`, `cargo clippy` and `cargo test --workspace`, which no stub can
stand in for. The two verdicts each kind of run produces are different claims and are recorded
apart; see `docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log` for the last `--full` run.

`--shell` is pinned only as "one run, `bash`, no `-c`, no fingerprint". The interactive
session itself, and the `-it` flags that require a terminal, are not exercised: under the stub
the pseudo-terminal allocation is a string in argv, and claiming more than that from it would
be the kind of assertion that passes while the feature is broken.

== 8. Reproduce

    git clone --no-hardlinks --depth 1 . /var/tmp/container-entry-fixtures
    cd /var/tmp/container-entry-fixtures

    python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py
    bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test
    python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    bash scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only 'container runner fixtures'

The first two are the suites, the third is the rule that keeps the file wired, the fourth
needs the image and is the same command as in section 6 with a locally built tag. To watch the
defect itself rather than its fixture, check out the version of the entry point from before
this change and give it an empty pattern with a stub on `PATH`: the exit status is 0, the
verdict reads as a sweep, and the number of `docker run` invocations equals the size of the
quick list plus the preflight.
